WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.600
Leaving Mossville was difficult for me, very difficult.

2
00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:10.020
And we were comfortable there, we were closer to everything there.

3
00:00:10.020 --> 00:00:13.960
My child was raised . . . We were all there.

4
00:00:13.960 --> 00:00:18.300
Family close . . . neighbors . . . the closely knit community.

5
00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:20.760
We loved and took care of each other from the beginning.

6
00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:32.800
When I first went to Mossville it was so nice because blacks owned a lot of land and we didn’t see that in many places, okay?

7
00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:34.320
That was beautiful.

8
00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:40.220
And just to look around and feel the breeze going through the trees and hear the rustling of the trees.

9
00:00:40.220 --> 00:00:43.420
And not being so close to your neighbors, but close enough if you needed anyone.

10
00:00:43.420 --> 00:00:48.260
And to reach out . . . Like Miss Flo and I, we lived next door to each other.

11
00:00:48.260 --> 00:00:49.220
We didn’t have a fence.

12
00:00:49.220 --> 00:00:52.140
A lot of conversations, but no fence.

13
00:00:52.140 --> 00:00:56.580
On the other side there was this little fence with blackberries growing to the Bernards.

14
00:00:56.580 --> 00:01:02.820
And we could watch . . . We’d sit on the porch and watch them walking to church at the end of Prater Road to the Methodist church on the corner.

15
00:01:02.820 --> 00:01:05.640
She was in all white and there he was following his wife.

16
00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:08.420
And do you know they died within weeks of each other?

17
00:01:08.420 --> 00:01:09.860
They were just that close.

18
00:01:09.860 --> 00:01:11.860
It was just a beautiful thing.

19
00:01:11.860 --> 00:01:19.320
When our house burned in 1983, the community gave us a shower, okay?

20
00:01:19.320 --> 00:01:24.160
The family gave Dawn . . . Dawn was about seven or eight years old, her own shower at her grandmother’s house.

21
00:01:24.160 --> 00:01:25.800
This lady’s house [showing a photograph].

22
00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:27.200
They lived just a block down.

23
00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:30.200
Everybody lived a block . . . I mean you were so close to each other.

24
00:01:30.200 --> 00:01:33.780
We had grocery stores in the community.

25
00:01:33.780 --> 00:01:34.620
Conoco . . .

26
00:01:34.620 --> 00:01:34.820
Everybody took care of each other.

27
00:01:34.620 --> 00:01:34.820
You’re right.

28
00:01:34.620 --> 00:01:37.400
Conoco filling station at the corner.

29
00:01:37.400 --> 00:01:40.260
It was just a beautiful thing.

30
00:01:40.260 --> 00:01:44.280
And then when they came with the lights on Prater [Road], we were so excited!

31
00:01:44.280 --> 00:01:46.520
And running water, all of that.

32
00:01:46.520 --> 00:01:48.140
You lived through all of that.

33
00:01:48.140 --> 00:01:49.420
It was just a good place.

34
00:01:49.420 --> 00:01:53.740
They had parades and a parade down Prater Road from Reesie [Mae Watts] Mouton,

35
00:01:53.740 --> 00:02:00.860
a member of Mount Zion Baptist Church sponsored the parade that would come down Prater Road into Queensboro and all of that area.

36
00:02:00.860 --> 00:02:03.380
It was just a closely knit family.

37
00:02:03.380 --> 00:02:04.620
And the Mossville Pirates?

38
00:02:04.620 --> 00:02:07.940
Oh my God, that’s all you heard, okay.

39
00:02:07.940 --> 00:02:10.300
It was just good.

40
00:02:10.300 --> 00:02:11.620
So that I miss.

41
00:02:11.620 --> 00:02:18.200
And I’m telling you what when they said that, I started . . . It was a feeling of disbelief, then tears.

42
00:02:18.200 --> 00:02:24.960
I couldn’t stop crying just to look and see how my community was being dismantled, okay?

43
00:02:24.960 --> 00:02:27.080
And the prices they were offering.

44
00:02:27.080 --> 00:02:31.040
In other words, they’re . . . And I’m sure there were persons who were better off,

45
00:02:31.040 --> 00:02:36.280
but then those of us who had more got less in a sense, okay?

46
00:02:36.280 --> 00:02:38.860
So it was not done fairly.

47
00:02:38.860 --> 00:02:42.160
So we said, “Why don’t you use a running scale?”

48
00:02:42.160 --> 00:02:46.620
But it was like giving an offer and you either accept or you reject.
